+    /*
+     * glReadPixels advances output rows according to GL_PACK_* state, not the
+     * destination pixelmap's pm_row_bytes. Modern BRender memory pixelmaps
+     * align RGB888 rows to whole pixels (for example 782px => 2352 bytes),
+     * while OpenGL's default 4-byte pack alignment produces 2348 bytes for
+     * the same 782 RGB pixels. Writing with the latter stride and then walking
+     * the buffer with the former shears every subsequent row diagonally.
+     *
+     * If the destination has a positive whole-pixel stride, teach OpenGL the
+     * exact BRender row length and force byte alignment. Otherwise read into a
+     * tight temporary buffer and copy rows through DevicePixelmapMemAddress(),
+     * which also handles inverted/negative-stride memory pixelmaps correctly.
+     */
+    if(dest->pm_row_bytes > 0 &&
